## 8. std/hw/arch_x86/ — the third x86 backend

The std library contains a third x86 codegen path: `std/hw/arch_x86/x86_codegen.oo` (a constrained-subset compiler that emits raw x86 machine code bytes from .oo source strings), plus `x86_assembler.oo`, `x86_encoder.oo`, `x86_abi.oo`, `x86_regs.oo`, `x86_syscalls.oo`, `x86_linker.oo`, `x86_direct_elf.oo`, `x86_disasm_x86.oo`, and ~100 fixture files.

This is a separate, complete x86-64 toolchain that doesn't depend on `oodac emit-x86`. It is used by the std library to compile .oo source at runtime (e.g. for `verify_x86_direct_elf`, `verify_x86_5th`, etc.). It has its own ABI constants, register set, syscall table, encoder, assembler, and linker.

The `oodac emit-x86` flag does NOT call into `std/hw/arch_x86/`. The two are independent code paths. This means the x86 backend has **two parallel implementations** of similar functionality.

Recommendation: the std-level `x86_codegen` is the more principled one (real codegen, real ABI). The `oodac emit-x86` token templates should be deprecated and replaced with the std-level codegen, or with a call into it. Tier 5 #24 (AST-based pipeline) is the right vehicle.
